Subject: [PATCH] stamp-p4: esp32p4 drop the CLIC interrupt level at IRQ entry (#5)

Fix for the #5 latch, applied to every build (C68_IRQ_LEVEL0=0
leaves it out).  The latch is not SMP-only: with one CPU, killing a busy
task can leave CPU0 deaf until the MWDT resets the chip.

The E1 captures (0068 + 0077, maxcpus=2) show, on every deaf boot, the
same transition: CPU1's last accepted interrupt was taken in a user
task, and the next mret on CPU1 returned to a different task (a syscall
or fork return, written mcause INT=0).  No mret ever wrote a nonzero
MPIL (es_mret_bad_mpil=0), and after the transition CPU1 keeps taking
ecalls and returning with mret while interrupts stay pending, enabled
and unaccepted (clic23=ffc00101, st_raw=1).

Hypothesis: this CLIC restores mintstatus.mil from mcause.MPIL only on
an mret with mcause.INT=1.  Taking an interrupt raises mil to the
interrupt's level (0xff here).  When Linux returns from it through the
same frame, the exit shim keeps INT=1 and mil drops back to 0.  When the
interrupt instead ends in schedule() on the return to user, the next
mret on that hart belongs to another task (INT=0) or there is none at
all (a switch to a kernel thread or the idle task), so mil stays at 0xff
and every later interrupt is below the level.  The interrupted task's
own INT=1 return would clear it, but under SMP that task can resume on
the other CPU, and then the hart stays deaf for good.

Linux never nests interrupt levels: it handles every interrupt with
MIE=0.  So drop mil back to 0 as soon as the frame is saved, with an
mret to the next instruction that writes mcause INT=1, MPP=M, MPIE=0,
MPIL=0.  MIE stays 0 (from MPIE=0), mepc and mcause are rewritten from
pt_regs by the exit shim as before, and from here on nothing depends on
which mret ends the interrupt.

---
--- a/arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S
+++ b/arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S
@@ -256,6 +256,26 @@
 	bge s4, zero, 1f
 
 	/* Handle interrupts */
+#ifdef CONFIG_SOC_ESP32P4
+	/*
+	 * #5 (0079): the CLIC raised mintstatus.mil to this interrupt's
+	 * level on entry and only an mret with mcause.INT=1 brings it
+	 * back.  If this interrupt ends in schedule(), the next mret on
+	 * this hart belongs to another task (INT=0) or never comes (a
+	 * switch to a kernel thread or idle), and the hart stops taking
+	 * interrupts.  Drop the level now with an mret to the next
+	 * instruction: INT=1, MPP=M, MPIE=0 (MIE stays 0), MPIL=0.
+	 * Linux handles interrupts with MIE=0 and never nests levels;
+	 * mepc and mcause are rewritten from pt_regs on the way out.
+	 * t0 is free (saved in pt_regs).
+	 */
+	la   t0, .Lesp32p4_irq_level0
+	csrw CSR_EPC, t0
+	li   t0, (1 << 31) | (3 << 28)
+	csrw CSR_CAUSE, t0
+	mret
+.Lesp32p4_irq_level0:
+#endif
 	call do_irq
 	j ret_from_exception
 1:
